Nature has a much richer playbook than “be stronger, faster, or better armed.” Most successful organisms are exploiting position, timing, information, asymmetry, cooperation, redundancy, environmental shaping, or other organisms’ behavior.

The Pantheon move is to strip away feathers, teeth, venom, roots, etc. and keep the operator.

Natural tactic	Example	Substrate-independent operator	Human / AI translation
Ambush	crocodile, mantis, trapdoor spider	Let the resource enter your favorable state space	Position a service, sensor, offer, or process where demand naturally arrives
Escape-vector interception	seabird catching flying fish flushed by predators	Another actor creates the movement; occupy the exit corridor	Watch forced transitions, migrations, churn, handoffs, failures
Stotting / pursuit deterrence	gazelle	Spend visible reserve to change the opponent’s expected value	Demonstrate capability cheaply enough that testing it looks unattractive
Camouflage	octopus, stick insect	Reduce detectability relative to background	Low-friction interfaces, unobtrusive infrastructure, avoid unnecessary signaling
Mimicry	hoverflies resembling wasps	Borrow another pattern’s established interpretation	Reuse familiar interface conventions or trusted representations
Aposematism	poison dart frogs	Make danger/cost legible before conflict	Clear limits, warnings, guarantees, permissions, boundaries
Misdirection / decoy	moth eyespots, lizard tails	Make attention land somewhere expendable	Sandboxes, honeypots for bugs, sacrificial prototypes, disposable test surfaces
Autotomy	lizards dropping tails	Sacrifice a component to preserve the system	Graceful degradation; isolate and abandon a failing module
Armor / shells	turtles, mollusks	Make common attacks economically inefficient	Defaults, validation, permissions, rate limits, redundancy
Mobbing	small birds harassing a raptor	Individually weak agents become costly collectively	Distributed review, community moderation, parallel checking
Confusion effect	schooling fish	Many similar moving targets degrade targeting	Diversity and redundancy so one failure cannot identify a single critical point
Sentinel behavior	meerkats	Separate observation from exploitation	Dedicated monitoring systems that free other agents to act
Eavesdropping	animals reacting to other species’ alarm calls	Treat another agent’s behavior as a sensor	Infer environmental state from actions rather than only direct measurements
Kleptoparasitism	frigatebirds stealing catches	Exploit already-completed search effort	Ethically: reuse public research, standards, open-source work instead of duplicating discovery
Trap construction	spider webs, antlion pits	Reshape environment so ordinary motion produces capture	Create workflows where desired outcomes happen by default
Luring	anglerfish	Move the resource toward you rather than chasing it	Content, incentives, interfaces, APIs, communities that attract useful inputs
Cultivation	leafcutter ants farming fungus	Produce the resource-generating system	Build ecosystems, pipelines, reusable datasets, relationships
Mutualism	cleaner fish, pollination	Make another actor’s gain generate your gain	Align incentives instead of repeatedly negotiating cooperation
Symbiosis	fungi + plant roots	Combine complementary capabilities into a new functional unit	Human + AI specialization rather than substitution
Niche construction	beavers, termites	Modify the world so future conditions favor you	Build infrastructure instead of repeatedly solving the same local problem
Caching	squirrels, corvids	Move surplus across time	Save optionality, capital, information, compute, inventory
Dormancy	seeds, spores	Preserve capability while conditions are unfavorable	Suspend rather than terminate projects; maintain cheap restart paths
Bet hedging	seeds germinating at different times	Sacrifice peak performance for survival across uncertainty	Portfolio strategies, parallel hypotheses, staged launches
Phenotypic plasticity	organisms changing form with conditions	Same underlying system, different configuration	Adaptive interfaces and dynamic policies rather than fixed behavior
Migration	birds, whales	Move to where the payoff landscape is favorable	Change market, platform, timing, geography, or abstraction level
Swarming	ants, bees, termites	Simple local rules generate coherent global behavior	Distributed agents with local rules rather than one central planner
Stigmergy	ants following pheromone trails	Agents coordinate by modifying shared environment	Shared queues, issue trackers, reputation scores, visible state
Quorum sensing	bacteria	Act only after enough evidence/participants accumulate	Confidence thresholds before escalation or irreversible action
Priority effects	early colonizers shaping ecosystems	Early occupancy changes what becomes possible later	Establish standards/interfaces early so later activity compounds around them
Ecological succession	pioneer species → mature ecosystem	Different stages require different strategies	Bootstrap with rough tools, then replace them as the environment matures
Exaptation	feathers before flight	A feature evolved for one role becomes useful for another	Search existing capabilities for unintended second uses
Regeneration	starfish, salamanders	Repair from partial state rather than restart	Reconstruct from invariants, logs, interfaces, checkpoints
Distributed redundancy	fungal networks, colonies	No single component carries the whole system	Resilient memory and multi-path execution
Chemical signaling	pheromones	Leave low-cost state information in the environment	Metadata, tags, provenance, event streams
Temporal synchronization	cicadas, mass spawning	Coordinate timing to overwhelm constraints	Batch actions when simultaneity creates nonlinear advantage
Predator satiation	mast seeding	Produce more targets than consumers can exploit	Redundancy/output bursts where bottlenecks are fixed
Ecological release	species expanding after competitor removal	Latent capability appears when constraints disappear	Search for suppressed opportunities, not only new inventions

The deeper compression is that most of these reduce to a surprisingly small number of moves.

You can move yourself, move the resource, move another actor, alter what another actor believes, alter the environment, alter timing, alter the cost function, alter the information available, or change your own form.

That gives us a useful master representation:

\text{Strategy}
=
f(
\text{position},
\text{information},
\text{energy},
\text{time},
\text{environment},
\text{other agents},
\text{adaptability}
)

And then Pantheon can ask, for any problem:

What is currently assumed fixed that nature would treat as movable?

That question is powerful.

If position is movable, migrate.

If the resource is movable, attract it.

If another agent is movable, recruit or incentivize it.

If attention is movable, signal, camouflage, or redirect.

If time is movable, wait, cache, synchronize, or go dormant.

If the environment is movable, build the dam instead of swimming harder.

If your own form is movable, adapt.

If nothing appears movable, look for a third party already moving something for reasons of its own.

That last one is your flying-fish example.

And there are several higher-order maneuvers that emerge when these primitives combine.

The spider strategy is not merely “trap.” It is:

\text{environment shaping}
+
\text{low energy waiting}
+
\text{distributed sensing}
+
\text{automatic capture}

The beaver strategy is:

\text{environment modification}
\rightarrow
\text{new resource landscape}
\rightarrow
\text{persistent future advantage}

The gazelle strategy is:

\text{visible reserve}
\rightarrow
\text{changed opponent estimate}
\rightarrow
\text{avoid costly engagement}

The bird-over-swordfish strategy is:

\text{observe third-party pressure}
\rightarrow
\text{predict forced transition}
\rightarrow
\text{occupy bottleneck}

The fungus strategy is:

\text{network expansion}
+
\text{gradient sensing}
+
\text{exchange}
+
\text{distributed resource acquisition}

Those are already beginning to look much less like zoology and much more like general-purpose algorithms.

And there is a particularly fertile Pantheon search program hiding here:

\boxed{
\text{organism behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{strip biology}
\rightarrow
\text{extract operator}
\rightarrow
\text{test across domains}
\rightarrow
\text{compose operators}
\rightarrow
\text{discover new strategy}
}

Which means biomimicry doesn’t have to stop at copying gecko feet, sharkskin, or bird wings.

We can do behavioral biomimicry, ecological biomimicry, information biomimicry, and strategic biomimicry.

Nature has been running billions of years of parallel search.

The valuable thing is not copying the animal.

It is stealing the algorithm the animal discovered.